<%# locals: (authority:, entity:) %>

<%# The permission half of one authority's block: are we currently allowed to
    file, where do we go to file, and the buttons to grant or revoke it.

    Separate from the fields above only because the Disconnect button is a
    button_to, which renders a <form>, and forms cannot nest inside the entity
    form. Everything else about this authority is saved by the page's one Save.

    Only once a taxpayer is chosen — there is nothing to authorise until we know
    whose books these are. %>

<% return unless (taxpayer = authority[:taxpayer]) %>

<%# The Connect button below starts an OAuth round trip, and the callback that
    ends it may need browser-collected values that only a script can gather. So
    the collector has to be running BEFORE the button is pressed, which is why
    the marker sits on this page and not only on the filing page. Named by the
    connector — no view knows which authority wants what. %>
<section id="<%= authority[:anchor] %>"
         data-browser-collector="<%= authority[:browser_collector] %>">
  <h2><%= t("filing.connection_title", authority: authority[:name]) %></h2>

  <% if taxpayer.connected? %>
    <p class="hint">
      <%= t("filing.connected_label",
            expires: taxpayer.token_expires_at && "#{l(taxpayer.token_expires_at, format: :short_date)} #{taxpayer.token_expires_at.strftime('%H:%M')}") %>
    </p>
    <% authority[:groups].each do |group| %>
      <% if (path = scheme_filing_path(entity, group.tax_scheme)) %>
        <%# The SUBMISSION name, not the scheme label: what gets sent is an MTD
            quarterly update, not the SA105 it eventually feeds. No authority
            either — this link sits inside a section headed "Connection to HMRC". %>
        <%= link_to t("filing.scheme_submission",
                      scheme: group.submission_name),
                    path, class: "backend_link button green" %><br /><br />
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
    <%= button_to t("filing.disconnect", authority: authority[:name]),
                  filing_disconnect_entity_path(entity,
                    connector: authority[:connector], taxpayer_id: taxpayer.id),
                  method: :delete,
                  data: { sure: t("filing.disconnect_confirm",
                                  authority: authority[:name], entity: entity.name) },
                  class: "backend_link button red" %>
  <% else %>
    <p class="hint"><%= t("filing.not_connected_label") %></p>
    <%# A NEW TAB, and the dashboard's Connect link does the same.
        Two reasons, both about what happens when the login does NOT go smoothly:

        · From the authority's own site there is no way back. Browser Back lands
          on filing#connect, which generates a fresh state and redirects to the
          authority again, so it loops. Abandoning the login stranded you.
        · This link sits OUTSIDE the form above and below its Save button, so
          leaving the page discarded anything unsaved in it, silently.

        The cost, accepted deliberately: the callback lands in the NEW tab, so
        afterwards the app is open twice and this tab still says "not connected"
        until it is reloaded. That is visible and a reload fixes it, which is
        better than a trap the user cannot see. Do not try to close the tab from
        the callback or message between tabs — JS complexity for something a
        reload already solves.

        The session is shared across tabs, so the OAuth state, the entity and the
        chosen taxpayer survive the round trip exactly as before. %>
    <%= link_to t("filing.connect", authority: authority[:name]),
                filing_connect_entity_path(entity,
                  connector: authority[:connector], taxpayer_id: taxpayer.id),
                target: "_blank", rel: "noopener",
                class: "backend_link button green" %>
  <% end %>
</section>
